<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Convert, then recruit. A child of Convert the way "reduce resistance only" is a child of Recruit, so it
    sits under it in the prisoner tab and reads as a variant of it rather than a mode of its own.

    listOrder 29 puts it directly after Convert at 28, which is what makes it draw indented beneath it.

    allowInClassicIdeoMode is false for the same reason Convert's is: there is no conversion to do when
    ideoligions are switched off, and a mode that can never make progress should not be offered.

    hideIfNotRecruitable is true because this one ends in a recruitment. Convert itself does not carry that
    flag, and correctly so: converting a prisoner you can never recruit is still worth doing.

    MayRequire is what keeps it out of a game without Ideology. Vanilla has no "requires this DLC" flag on the
    def itself and does not need one, because its own Convert lives inside the Ideology data folder and so is
    simply absent. Ours cannot be, so it says so here instead. allowInClassicIdeoMode covers the other case,
    where Ideology is installed and ideoligions are switched off.

    Note for whoever edits this comment: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment. An earlier draft used
    one as a dash, the whole file failed to parse, and the def silently did not exist.
  -->
  <PrisonerInteractionModeDef MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Ideology">
    <defName>Gideon_ConvertThenRecruit</defName>
    <label>recruit</label>
    <isChildInteraction>true</isChildInteraction>
    <listOrder>29</listOrder>
    <description>Wardens will attempt to convert the prisoner to their ideoligion, then switch to recruiting them once the conversion succeeds. A converted prisoner is far easier to recruit, so this is the patient route to a new colonist.</description>
    <allowInClassicIdeoMode>false</allowInClassicIdeoMode>
    <hideIfNotRecruitable>true</hideIfNotRecruitable>
  </PrisonerInteractionModeDef>

</Defs>
